[Music]  shares of gamestop a struggling video  game retailer were worth just under  three dollars earlier in the pandemic  but within weeks in 2021 they went from  17  to a peak of 468 and  most of those gains happened in just one  week one of the wildest moments in wall  street history  gamestop shot up as much as 500 percent  even as many wall street professionals  had bet it would fall to near zero  because pretty much their business is in  terminal decline that whole  drama really accelerated within the span  of a week which just shows you  how quickly these events can play out  when so many people are  talking about it online what makes this  rally unique  is that it was driven by individual  investors using apps like robinhood  and watching youtube videos tweets from  celebrities like elon musk  and the now famous wall street bets  reddit page  wall street bets on reddit gamestop what  is going going on in the reddit  community now that is  insane you're seeing a huge number of  people  on these commission free trading apps  able to make trades just from their  mobile phone really quickly  and a huge number of people discussing  these trades on social media  together i think those two forces  combined to  produce what we've been seeing the last  week which is basically this social  media driven trading frenzy  we looked at how the game stopped  trading craze spread online and turned  wall street upside down during one  wild week in january gamestop's frantic  trading week  started on the website reddit people  have gathered on a popular page  or subreddit called wall street bets  since 2012  but on january 25th the subreddit's most  popular post  was this gme yolo update  gme is gamestop's stock ticker symbol  and yolo means you only live once  the post had a screenshot of an  investment summary from a massachusetts  resident named keith gill  or as he's known on reddit dfv short for  something we  can't say in this video he also goes by  roaring kitty on youtube  this was a true yolo for me when when i  was building this position  last year we had nowhere close to a  million dollars  cheers everybody the screenshot from the  34 year old who until recently worked in  marketing  showed that he had more than 13 million  dollars in gamestop stocks  options and cash the replies to the post  were largely praising gill  some saying they're jumping in to buy  gamestop shares as well with  a lot of rocket emojis to keep all hands  and feet inside the rocket as they  launched gamestop stock to the moon  much of the gamestop trading wasn't just  buying and selling shares  but also a lot of complex transactions  such as options contracts  which allow investors to bet on how the  price of a stock will rise or  fall there was so much activity that  gamestop trading was halted multiple  times that day  the company's stock actually closed  lower at 76 dollars but  stood out as the most heavily traded  stock in the s p 500  on january 26 gamestop got a huge boost  tesla ceo elon musk chimed in tweeting  gamestunk with a link to the wall street  bets subreddit  stonk is a term widely used by online  investors to talk about stock trading  the tweet was liked over 251 000 times  and got more than 38 000 retweets  elon musk's tweet was kind of another  one of those  unbelievable moments in this whole saga  because you suddenly saw everyone  talking about this  like celebrities you know business  owners  executives people you thought would  never be  following this forum and following this  gamestop drama  another popular tweet came from chamoth  pauli hapatia  a venture capitalist and former facebook  executive  he said he bought calls on gamestop  meaning he was betting the share price  would continue to rise  by the time markets closed on tuesday  gamestop's share price was up to 147  dollars  and on january 27th i think that what  you're seeing  is um essentially a push back  against the establishment in a really  important way  gme went mainstream making headlines  around the world  social media average joes versus big  money players video game retailer  gamestop is set to continue their head  spinning ascent today  it's wall street's david versus goliath  that whole frenzy kind of  caught the attention of a lot of people  i mean i talked to some people  on twitter and on reddit who had never  traded before but they just saw what was  happening with gamestop and  they sort of couldn't resist it was kind  of like a fomo moment  the wall street bets reddit page  temporarily went private with  moderators citing technical difficulties  on an unprecedented scale as a result of  the newfound interest in wsb  the stock closed at 347  but then robin hood just absolutely  screwed over all the retail investors  today this is madness  this is absolute madness on january 28th  the popular trading platform robin hood  restricted trading of hot stocks  including gamestop giving users only two  options  hold or sell but not buy gamestop shares  plummeted  robinhood said it restricted some  trading on its platform because it  needed more money to pay its  clearinghouse  which processes trades on the back end  due to increased activity  other brokerage firms like weeble  financial and e-trade had also  restricted trading of gamestop  citing challenges in providing their  normal services  robin hood got a lot of backlash as its  brand is built around the idea of making  investment more accessible  that was i think a real flashpoint  i think it really added to the sense of  anger and the sort of victim mentality  that was growing because  a lot of these individuals who had just  seen these stocks doing really well  tried to get in on the action and then  saw overnight that they were unable to  do so  politicians across the aisle including  alexandria ocasio-cortez  and ted cruz tweeted their support for a  hearing on robin hood's decision to  restrict  trading at markets close gamestop's  share price settled around 193 dollars  after peaking at roughly 468.  at the end of the week gamestop stock  was worth 328 dollars  that's up 1600 in 2021.  it's been crazy out there what started  off as a unique market strategy  has evolved into class warfare and a  full-on market rebellion  gme trading eventually resumed on robin  hood though it was still limited  and gamestop shares rose again wall  street bets was full of memes  investment screenshots and members  celebrating the fact that they made a  real impact on wall street  in an exclusive interview with the wall  street journal keith gill  said he didn't expect this story to get  so big i think  there was a real acknowledgement of how  surreal this moment was that  wall street bats had become the center  of this drama  and had actually become this serious  thing that people were talking about  because a lot of the users would say  that it's sort of  it started out as a joke in just one  week wall street bets membership had  grown from 2.1 million  to 6.4 million and google searches for  gamestop had nearly doubled  what a crazy thing to be looking at here  this is gamestop's chart  looking at the daily just absolute  insanity the dramatic trading frenzy has  also brought scrutiny from the white  house  congress and the sec with planned  hearings and  calls for an investigation and tighter  regulation  and robin hood faced a number of class  actions from users over its trading  restrictions  gamestop is still mostly a traditional  brick and mortar company  selling video games and consoles in  malls and by february 2nd  its stock value had dropped to 90  dollars  while some redditors made millions  trading gamestop  other retail investors who bought when  the price was high are likely nursing  big losses  but gamestop has changed the debate  about the democratization of the stock  market and  the power of individual investors online  with wall street bets newfound following  the reddit crowd has already targeted  other stocks and assets and demonstrated  that it can have an  impact on markets i think for me the  biggest takeaway is that you cannot  ignore the individual it's something to  behold and it teaches  us that this moment can sort of  replicate itself in many other ways like  we it doesn't have to just be  a game stop we've already seen it's it's  moved on to silver prices  it's moved on to other things that you  wouldn't have necessarily thought about

